## 8. Rollback and recovery

Unchanged from revision 6, and re-proved against PostgreSQL.

- One run is still one transaction. The new refusal is an ERROR issue and
  reaches `rollback()` by the same route as every other one.
- A blocked run commits nothing — characters, versions, mappings and audit
  events all roll back together, checked pairwise rather than by row count so a
  silent re-key could not hide behind identical counts.
- An applied run remains recoverable by restoring the backup taken before it,
  per `docs/operations/database-development.md`. The Sheet is untouched and
  remains authoritative throughout Phase 2, so re-importing after a restore is
  safe.
- Reverting this correction requires no data repair: it changes comparison
  behaviour only, and wrote nothing to any database beyond the disposable
  `freedom_test`, which is left migrated and empty.

